<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ganden-phodrang">Glossary</a> · Term · government</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ganden Phodrang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1642">1642</a>–<a href="/years/1959">1959</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Ganden Podrang, Gaden Phodrang</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganden-monastery">Ganden</a> Phodrang was the Tibetan system of government established by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a> in 1642, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oirats">Oirat</a> lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a>, founder of the Khoshut Khanate, conferred all spiritual and political power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> on him at a ceremony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shigatse">Shigatse</a>. It took its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lamas</a>' residence at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drepung-monastery">Drepung Monastery</a>: Ganden is the Tibetan name for the Tushita heaven and Phodrang means a palace. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> became the capital and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school held authority. From 1720 Tibet was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of the Qing, who created the governing council, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashag">Kashag</a>, in 1721. The Ganden Phodrang operated in Lhasa until 1959, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/14th-dalai-lama">14th Dalai Lama</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> during the Tibetan uprising.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ganden-phodrang.jpg" alt="Ganden Phodrang" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: René Heise · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lhasa_Potala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1577 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altan-khan">Altan Khan</a> of the Tümed Mongols, who had chosen the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, invited its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sonam-gyatso">Sonam Gyatso</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and gave him the title Dalai, a Mongolian translation of Gyatso, meaning ocean. As the title was also given posthumously to two earlier incarnations, Sonam Gyatso counted as the 3rd Dalai Lama. The name Ganden Phodrang first referred to the quarters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> lineage at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drepung-monastery">Drepung Monastery</a> from the time of the 2nd Dalai Lama.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a> unified the Tibetan heartland under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school after a long civil war against the rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonang">Jonang</a> sects and the secular ruler, the Tsangpa prince, with help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a>. Historians call the civil administration he and his circle set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a> state. Güshi Khan did not interfere in it, and all power lay with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a> until his death. He began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potala-palace">Potala Palace</a> on the site of Lhasa's Red Fort and moved the government there from Drepung.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From 1679 to 1684 the Ganden Phodrang fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet-ladakh-mughal-war">Tibet–Ladakh–Mughal War</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namgyal-dynasty">Namgyal dynasty</a> of Ladakh; after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">5th Dalai Lama</a>'s death in 1682 the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangye-gyatso">Desi Sangye Gyatso</a> ended it with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tingmosgang">Treaty of Tingmosgang</a>. Tibet was drawn into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungar-qing-wars">Dzungar–Qing Wars</a>, and in 1705 the Qing conspired with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> faction to kidnap the 6th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>. In 1717 Dzungar forces killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhazang-khan">Lha-bzang Khan</a>, the last khan of the Khoshut Khanate, before the Qing expelled them.</p><p>Under Qing protection the Tibetan leader Pholhane reorganised the administration and army after 1727. When his son Gyurme Namgyal tried to expel the Qing troops, two ambans murdered him in 1750, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> reorganised the government in 1751. After the wars with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, the Twenty-Nine Article Imperial Ordinance of 1793 strengthened the ambans and introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-urn">Golden Urn</a>. Qing power weakened in the 19th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dogras">Dogra</a> forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> in 1841–1842.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the fall of the Qing, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thubten-gyatso">13th Dalai Lama</a> declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> independent, and Tibet is regarded as having been a de facto independent state until its annexation by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-republic-of-china">People's Republic of China</a> in 1951. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashag">Kashag</a> was formally dissolved after the 1959 uprising. Some see the Central Tibetan Administration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as the Ganden Phodrang's continuation, but in exile the name refers to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>'s personal office, his labrang.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ganden Phodrang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tibet was governed by the <b>Ganden Phodrang</b>, the government of the Dalai Lamas, with the Kashag as its council of ministers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet (1912-1951) · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In Tibet the Dalai Lama headed a government, the <b>Ganden Phodrang</b>, in which a council of ministers, the Kashag, and parallel ranks of monk and lay officials administered the country.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · How It Works</a></li><li><blockquote>The 5th Dalai Lama, who is credited with unifying Tibet under the <b>Ganden Phodrang</b>, proclaimed Lhasa the capital once more, in a move that recalled Songtsen Gampo.</blockquote><a href="/work/potala-palace">Potala Palace · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
